The clinical picture strongly points towards PCOS. In PCOS, particularly in obese patients, increased peripheral conversion of androgens (produced under LH stimulation) to estrogens (like estrone) in adipose tissue contributes to the hormonal milieu. This excess estrogen can exert feedback effects that further disrupt the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian (HPO) axis, often leading to increased GnRH pulse frequency, which favors LH secretion over FSH secretion, resulting in the characteristic high LH levels. Therefore, increased estrogen production in adipose tissue is a key factor contributing to the pathophysiology and the hormonal profile seen in this patient.

