**Conclusion:**
The patient has mild hyperkalemia (K+ 5.3) and evidence of renal dysfunction (BUN 33, Cr 1.8) in the context of CKD. While the hyperkalemia isn't immediately life-threatening, it needs management. IV fluids can help improve renal perfusion, potentially lower potassium, and address possible dehydration contributing to the worsening renal function. Calcium gluconate is reserved for more severe hyperkalemia. Hemodialysis is too aggressive at this stage. Therefore, administering IV fluids is the most appropriate next step among the choices provided to address the renal dysfunction and mild hyperkalemia.<unused95>A
