    *   **C: Osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS), formerly Central Pontine Myelinolysis (CPM):** A neurological disorder caused by rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia. The rapid shift in osmolarity leads to water moving out of brain cells, particularly oligodendrocytes in the pons (and sometimes other areas like the thalamus, basal ganglia, cerebellum, cortex), causing demyelination. Classic symptoms include dysarthria, dysphagia, quadriparesis, altered consciousness, and sometimes locked-in syndrome. The timing (days after rapid correction) and symptoms (quadriparesis, dysarthria) strongly match ODS. The initial hyponatremia (Na 118) and rapid correction (increase of 11 mEq/L in 6 hours) are classic risk factors.
