2.  **Connect CF to the symptoms:** Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder affecting the CFTR protein, primarily impacting epithelial cells in the lungs, pancreas, intestines, liver, and sweat glands. Pancreatic insufficiency is a common complication of CF, leading to malabsorption of fats and fat-soluble vitamins. Steatorrhea (greasy stools) is a classic sign of fat malabsorption due to pancreatic insufficiency.

3.  **Connect steatorrhea/malabsorption to coagulation factors:** Fat malabsorption can lead to deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins, specifically Vitamin K. Vitamin K is crucial for the synthesis of several coagulation factors in the liver. These factors are II (prothrombin), VII, IX, and X.

